\section{Media}{

A state can hold images, audio, video and PDFs next to text. A media object
(\code{\link[=img]{img()}}, \code{\link[=audio_file]{audio_file()}}, \code{\link[=video_file]{video_file()}}, \code{\link[=pdf_file]{pdf_file()}}) is an item, and so is
an \code{LLMMessage} (the text of its last user message, then its media). To put
several parts into one item, wrap them in a list inside \code{.state}:
\code{list(list(photo_a = img("a.jpg"), photo_b = img("b.jpg")))}. Names inside the
item label the parts, so a question can say "the animal in photo_a". The
providers read:

\tabular{lll}{
Provider \tab Reads \tab Image option\cr
\code{jev()} \tab image \tab none\cr
\code{openai()} \tab image \tab \code{.detail}\cr
\code{gemini()} \tab image, audio, video, PDF \tab \code{.resolution}\cr
\code{ollama()} \tab image (not yet tested against a live server) \tab none
}

\code{provider_capabilities(.what = "decide_media")} gives the same table as a
tibble. A provider that cannot read a media type stops with a message before
any request; \code{decide()} also checks image size, format and count against the
provider's limits and never resizes an image. A PDF sent to a provider that
does not read PDFs is replaced by its extracted text; a PDF made with
\code{pdf_file(.text_extract = TRUE)} always goes as text. Either way \code{decide()}
tells you once per call, naming the provider and the files, that layout,
tables and figures are lost. A PDF without extractable text stops the call.

Images, audio and video leave your machine and go to the provider. Do not send
restricted or personal data, such as survey photos with faces.
}

\section{Many states}{

Every element of \code{.state} is one item. An item is a string, a media object, an
\code{LLMMessage}, or a list of parts that belong together (strings and media
objects, one level only; names label the parts). \code{.state} itself is one item
passed bare, a character vector (one item per string), or a list of items (one
item per element). So \code{list(img("a.jpg"), img("b.jpg"))} is two items, and
\code{list(list("Caption", img("c.jpg")))} is one item with two parts.

A single item, however it is given, returns the plain tibble. Two or more items
give one result tibble with a leading \code{state} column (the item's name when the
outer list is named, else its text cut to 60 characters or a short description
of its media) and an integer \code{state_id}, then the usual columns.

The requests run in parallel, at most \code{.max_active} at a time, and are retried
like a single request. A state that fails after its retries, or that the model
refuses, gives NA rows and one warning; the other states are kept. A malformed
item stops the call before any request and names its position.
}

